    overview: 'This is a rebuild of PokemonRedExperiments, the well-known project that trains PPO to play Pokemon Red, to see whether a cleaner reward and a custom training stack could beat that baseline. The agent sees what a player sees: the last three downscaled screens, a map of where it has already walked, its party levels and HP, its badges, how close it is to the stall limit, and the game\'s event flags. It picks one of seven buttons every 24 frames.\n\nEverything else is mine: the Gymnasium environment on PyBoy, a vectorized runner that steps hundreds of emulators in lockstep through shared memory, a custom PPO loop, atomic checkpoints with resume, and scripts that set up a Runpod A100 pod in one command, train for 400M steps and delete the pod when done.\n\nI ran nine training runs. Every finished run was audited from its W&B tables and from game states saved in stuck episodes and read back from RAM. For run6 and run9 I also traced 64 frozen episodes step by step. Each audit decided what the next run changed.',
    challenge: 'Pokemon Red gives no score. You choose what to pay for, and PPO finds every way to collect that pay without doing what you meant. Paying for healing or XP leads to grinding. A penalty computed as the change in a running total refunds itself.\n\nThe rule this project follows is that every reward term pays at most once for the same thing, so each term has a fixed maximum per episode and no loop of button presses earns more. That rule makes the agent hard to exploit, and it also makes the failures readable: whenever the next reward was out of reach, the policy stopped where it was last paid. run8 parked in Cerulean Gym for 95M steps. run5 kept pressing into the Fan Club table where it got the Bike Voucher, and run6 ended a median 94% of late episodes in that same building. Losing a fight cost nothing but money, so run6 treated blackouts as free heals and started its losing fights on the S.S. Anne at a median 14% HP.\n\nEvery fix had to make the next real step of the game worth taking without creating something new to farm. A full 400M-step run took 15-21 hours of A100 time, roughly $35-45, on one seed, so I couldn\'t answer questions by brute force. I had to answer them from evidence.',
    approach: 'Emulator memory is parsed once per step into a frozen GameFrame, and each reward term is a small object that sees only that frame, so all 14 terms are unit-tested without a ROM. Game addresses live in one RAM map. Facts the RAM doesn\'t hold, like a hidden sign being read or a tree being cut, come from PyBoy hooks on the game\'s own routines, checked against the pret/pokered disassembly.\n\nEpisodes end as terminated after 10,240 steps with no progress, and the agent can see how close that limit is. Before that rule, stalling cost nothing: running out of time doesn\'t count as the world ending, so the value is bootstrapped.\n\nLater runs start from earlier runs\' weights (--init-from), and a frozen-policy control tests what the policy does without learning during the episode. That control showed the first run\'s Misty win rate was 84% with updates and 39.5% without, so training-time numbers alone overstate what a policy has learned.\n\nThe audits read RAM from stuck states, and for run6 and run9 they trace 64 episodes of the final checkpoint. For run6 I also replayed decisive fights under controlled conditions: replaying its 15 S.S. Anne rival fights fully healed won 119 of 120, against 11 of 120 as saved. The policy could win the fight, but the fight paid nothing, so the agent arrived too weak to win it.',
